🧑‍💻 AWS IAM (Identity and Access Management) – Deep Dive Notes
🌍 1. What is AWS IAM?

IAM (Identity and Access Management) is a global AWS service used to securely control access to AWS resources.

👉 It helps you manage who can do what in your AWS account.

🧩 Example:

You (Admin) can create:

👤 Users – individual people or services

👥 Groups – collection of users

🔐 Policies – permissions attached to users/groups

🎭 Roles – temporary credentials for AWS resources (like EC2 → S3 access)

🧠 2. Why is IAM Important?
Feature	Description
🔒 Security	Controls access using least privilege
⚙️ Flexibility	Create fine-grained permissions
🌐 Centralized Management	One place to manage all identities
🔁 Temporary Access	Via IAM Roles
🧩 Integration	Works with every AWS service
🏗️ 3. Core Components of IAM
Component	Description
Users	Individual accounts (e.g., Developer1, Admin)
Groups	Collection of users (e.g., DevTeam, OpsTeam)
Roles	Assign temporary permissions to AWS resources
Policies	JSON documents defining permissions
Identity Provider (IdP)	For SSO (e.g., Google, Azure AD)
🧰 4. IAM Users

Each user gets Access key and Secret key (for CLI/SDK).

Users can be assigned policies directly or via groups.

🪜 Create IAM User:

Go to IAM → Users → Add User

Enter user name: developer1

Access type:

✅ Console access (password)

✅ Programmatic access (CLI)

Attach policy: AmazonS3FullAccess

Create user ✅

👥 5. IAM Groups

Groups simplify permission management for multiple users.

Example:

Group: Developers

Policy: AmazonEC2FullAccess

Add users: dev1, dev2

All users in the group inherit permissions from that policy.

🧾 6. IAM Policies

Policies define permissions in JSON format.

Example: Allow full access to S3
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "s3:*",
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}

Example: Allow only EC2 start/stop
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "ec2:StartInstances",
        "ec2:StopInstances"
      ],
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}

🔐 7. IAM Password Policy

You can enforce a strong password policy for all users.

🪜 Set Password Policy:

Go to IAM → Account Settings → Password Policy

Enable:

Minimum length (e.g., 8)

Require uppercase/lowercase

Require numbers and symbols

Password expiration (90 days)

Prevent password reuse

This improves account-level security 🔒

📱 8. AWS Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)
🧩 What is MFA?

An extra layer of security beyond password.

User must provide:

Password

MFA code (from device)

🪜 Enable MFA:

IAM → Users → Select User → Security Credentials

Assign MFA device:

Virtual MFA (Google Authenticator / Authy)

Hardware MFA device

Scan QR → Enter 2 codes → Enable ✅

Now login requires OTP each time 🔐

💻 9. AWS CLI (Command Line Interface)

CLI allows you to manage AWS resources using terminal commands.

🪜 Install AWS CLI (Linux):
sudo apt install awscli -y

🪜 Configure AWS CLI:
aws configure


Enter:

AWS Access Key ID [None]: <your-access-key>
AWS Secret Access Key [None]: <your-secret-key>
Default region name [None]: ap-south-1
Default output format [None]: json


Now your CLI is connected ✅

🧠 Example Commands:
Command	Description
aws s3 ls	List all S3 buckets
aws ec2 describe-instances	Show all EC2 instances
aws iam list-users	List all IAM users
aws ec2 start-instances --instance-ids i-123456	Start EC2 instance
aws ec2 stop-instances --instance-ids i-123456	Stop EC2 instance
🧑‍🤝‍🧑 10. Handle Multiple AWS Accounts in CLI

You can configure multiple profiles.

🪜 Add New Profile:
aws configure --profile devaccount


To use that profile:

aws s3 ls --profile devaccount


💡 Each profile can have different keys and regions.

🎭 11. AWS IAM Roles

Roles are like temporary users for AWS services.

Example:

Allow EC2 instance to access S3 without using Access Keys.

🪜 Steps:

IAM → Roles → Create Role

Trusted Entity: EC2

Attach Policy: AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess

Launch EC2 → Attach this Role

Now your EC2 can run:

aws s3 ls


✅ without any access key!

☁️ 12. AWS CloudShell

CloudShell = browser-based terminal to run AWS CLI commands directly.

Features:

Pre-authenticated (no need to configure credentials)

Linux shell environment

Can run aws, git, python3, etc.

To use:

Click CloudShell icon in top menu of AWS Console.

🧾 13. IAM Access Advisor

Shows which services each user has recently used.

Helps remove unused permissions (principle of least privilege).

To view:

Go to IAM → Users → Access Advisor

See service usage history.

🧾 14. IAM Credential Report

CSV file showing all users, password last used, MFA enabled, etc.

Helps in security auditing.

To generate:

IAM → Credential Report → Download Report

Opens CSV file with:

user, mfa_active, password_last_used, etc.

☁️ Amazon S3 (Simple Storage Service)
🧩 15. Introduction to S3

Amazon S3 is an object storage service to store and retrieve any amount of data from anywhere on the internet.

🔹 Key Features:
Feature	Description
Infinite storage	Store unlimited data
Highly durable	99.999999999% (11 9’s) durability
Scalable	Automatically scales with your needs
Secure	Supports IAM, encryption, versioning
Cost-effective	Pay only for what you use
📦 16. S3 Basic Concepts
Term	Description
Bucket	Container for objects (files)
Object	File + Metadata
Key	Object name inside bucket
Region	Buckets are region specific
Versioning	Keeps multiple versions of same file
Storage Classes	Different cost & durability levels
🪜 17. Create S3 Bucket (Step-by-Step)

Go to S3 → Create Bucket

Enter unique name: my-demo-bucket-ashu

Choose Region: ap-south-1

Uncheck “Block all public access” (optional)

Create bucket ✅

Upload files manually or using CLI.

💻 18. AWS CLI – S3 Commands
Command	Description
aws s3 ls	List all buckets
aws s3 mb s3://my-bucket	Create bucket
aws s3 cp file.txt s3://my-bucket/	Upload file
aws s3 cp s3://my-bucket/file.txt .	Download file
aws s3 rm s3://my-bucket/file.txt	Delete file
aws s3 sync . s3://my-bucket/	Sync local → S3
🧰 19. S3 Storage Classes
Class	Use Case
Standard	Frequently accessed data
Standard-IA	Infrequently accessed
One Zone-IA	Single AZ data
Glacier	Archival storage (minutes to hours retrieval)
Glacier Deep Archive	Long-term backup
Intelligent-Tiering	Auto moves between classes
🔐 20. S3 Security
Feature	Description
IAM Policies	Control who can access S3
Bucket Policies	Public/private control
ACLs	Legacy access controls
Encryption	SSE-S3 / SSE-KMS / SSE-C
Versioning	Protect from accidental delete
MFA Delete	Prevent unauthorized deletion
♻️ 21. S3 Versioning

Keeps multiple versions of the same file.

aws s3api put-bucket-versioning --bucket my-bucket --versioning-configuration Status=Enabled


Now if you upload same file again → old version still available.

📦 22. S3 Lifecycle Policies

Automate storage transitions or deletions.

Example:

Move files from Standard → Glacier after 30 days.

Delete after 1 year.

🌎 23. S3 Public Access & Static Website Hosting
🪜 Host a Static Website:

Upload HTML files

Enable Static Website Hosting

Set permissions:

{
  "Version":"2012-10-17",
  "Statement":[{
    "Effect":"Allow",
    "Principal":"*",
    "Action":"s3:GetObject",
    "Resource":"arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/*"
  }]
}


Access website at:

http://my-bucket.s3-website.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com

🧠 24. IAM + S3 Integration (Real-World Use Case)

Scenario:

Developer should upload files to S3 but not delete them.

Create Policy:

{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:PutObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket", "arn:aws:s3:::my-bucket/*"]
    }
  ]
}


Attach to Developer IAM user ✅

